00:01:15.662 --> 00:01:21.662
University of Liege, astrophysicists, Fizz.org reports, built the Destiny model using

00:01:21.662 --> 00:01:26.502
Markov chains with absorbing states to track molecules on amorphous ice surfaces.

00:01:26.922 --> 00:01:32.262
A Markov chain is a step-by-step model that tracks the chance of each next move, and this

00:01:32.262 --> 00:01:37.762
one assigns binding energies, how tightly a molecule sticks, to individual surface sites

00:01:37.762 --> 00:01:39.622
instead of a single average value.

00:01:40.262 --> 00:01:44.942
Encounters can change that binding strength without a reaction, and methane population

00:01:44.942 --> 00:01:47.982
trends already differ from earlier single energy models.

00:01:47.982 --> 00:01:51.422
The approach was detailed in astronomy and astrophysics.

00:01:52.571 --> 00:01:57.271
Molecules arriving from the gas phase can land on a sticky site or a slippery one on that

00:01:57.271 --> 00:01:58.011
uneven ice.

00:01:58.771 --> 00:02:04.311
Each adsorbed species, a molecule stuck on the surface, faces four possible outcomes at

00:02:04.311 --> 00:02:09.491
every step, staying in place, diffusing to a new site, reacting, or disorbing.

00:02:10.191 --> 00:02:15.431
That treatment reproduces the observed competition between adsorption, diffusion, and desorption

00:02:15.431 --> 00:02:17.091
on realistic, disordered ice.

00:02:17.091 --> 00:02:23.871
Direct comparison with new James Webb Space Telescope, or JWST, spectra of ice features

00:02:23.871 --> 00:02:28.991
and dense clouds would test whether the site-by-site treatment is required, or whether simpler

00:02:28.991 --> 00:02:31.791
averages still suffice at the precision now achievable.

00:02:32.431 --> 00:02:37.491
What remains unknown is how large the cumulative effect becomes once many species and many

00:02:37.491 --> 00:02:39.111
grain types are included together.

